From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:07:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] irqchip/apple-aic: take T8132 device IRQs on the E
 cluster by default

On the J604 the boot chain leaves only CPU 0, a P core, accepting AIC
IRQs, so every device interrupt wakes the P cluster. macOS takes most
device IRQs on the E cores.

Measured on the J604 at idle, display on, with t8132_irq_cluster written
at runtime: with IRQs on cluster 0 (the E cluster, CPUs 1-6) the system
draws 160 mW less under the balanced profile, and 1.19 W less under
performance (mean of two alternating cells per setting after a settle),
almost all of it on the P cluster rail.

Make cluster 0 the default. -1 still leaves IRQs with the boot CPUs. When
the routing cannot be applied (not at EL2, or no boot IRQ CPU found), the
parameter now reads back -1 and the note is informational, since a
default is not a request.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/irqchip/irq-apple-aic.c | 11 +++++++----
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/irqchip/irq-apple-aic.c b/drivers/irqchip/irq-apple-aic.c
index d77f358cc3..6d8873be97 100644
--- a/drivers/irqchip/irq-apple-aic.c
+++ b/drivers/irqchip/irq-apple-aic.c
@@ -428,9 +428,11 @@ static struct cpumask aic_irq_cpus;
  * on E cores. t8132_irq_cluster=N moves delivery to the CPUs of cluster N
  * (MPIDR Aff1) once one of them is online, and gives it back to the boot CPUs
  * before the last of them goes offline, so some CPU always accepts IRQs.
- * Effective affinity is still reported as before. -1 (the default) is off.
+ * Effective affinity is still reported as before. The default is cluster 0,
+ * the E cluster, where macOS takes most device IRQs; -1 leaves them with the
+ * boot CPUs.
  */
-static int t8132_irq_cluster = -1;
+static int t8132_irq_cluster;
 
 static struct cpumask aic_route_boot_cpus;	/* CPUs that accepted IRQs as booted */
 static struct cpumask aic_route_cpus;		/* CPUs of the cluster now accepting */
@@ -474,7 +476,7 @@ static const struct kernel_param_ops t8132_irq_cluster_ops = {
 
 module_param_cb(t8132_irq_cluster, &t8132_irq_cluster_ops, &t8132_irq_cluster, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(t8132_irq_cluster,
-		 "T8132: CPU cluster (MPIDR Aff1) taking AIC IRQs (-1: the boot CPU, default)");
+		 "T8132: CPU cluster (MPIDR Aff1) taking AIC IRQs (default 0, the E cluster; -1: the boot CPU)");
 
 static void aic_handle_ipi(struct pt_regs *regs);
 
@@ -1614,8 +1616,9 @@ static int __init aic_route_init(void)
 		return 0;
 	if (!is_kernel_in_hyp_mode() || cpumask_empty(&aic_route_boot_cpus)) {
 		if (t8132_irq_cluster >= 0)
-			pr_warn("t8132_irq_cluster=%d ignored: not at EL2 or no boot IRQ CPU found\n",
+			pr_info("t8132_irq_cluster=%d not applied: not at EL2 or no boot IRQ CPU found\n",
 				t8132_irq_cluster);
+		t8132_irq_cluster = -1;
 		return 0;
 	}
 	guard(mutex)(&aic_route_param_lock);
-- 
